;; =============================================================================
;; Spec: Ariadne step 2a — ExecutionGrant, bounded authority as an object
;; =============================================================================
;;
;; Normative refs: docs/architecture/rfc-ariadne-adoption.md (adoption
;; step 2; ratified decision 3 — "N10 is subsumed, not extended");
;; Ariadne §13.5 (ExecutionGrant / Delegation, lineage, revocation for
;; cause); N10-governed-tool-execution.md §6 (Capability Model — the
;; same object under the older name).

{:spec/title "Ariadne step 2a: execution-grant component - bounded authority as a first-class object"

 :spec/description
 "Create component execution-grant: the object that carries authority
  to perform an effect, so authority stops being ambient.

  Today authority is ambient — an agent that is running can do what the
  process can do. `components/policy/` declares `check-budget`,
  `enforce-policy`, and `evaluate-gates` but has ZERO callers outside
  its own ns form (verified: grepping `ai.miniforge.policy.interface`
  across components/, bases/, and projects/ returns only the interface
  file itself). There is no object anywhere in the tree that says
  'this actor may do this effect, within these bounds, until this time.'

  N10 §6 already specifies this object as `:capability/*` — structure,
  verbs, limits, ttl-seconds, policy-bindings, revocation-handle. Per
  the RFC's ratified decision 3, N10 is SUBSUMED: this component is
  N10's capability re-expressed in Ariadne vocabulary, not a second
  parallel mechanism. N10's spec text gets rewritten as a profile of
  this interface in step 4; this spec builds the interface.

  GROUP 1: Schema (closed, like DecisionEnvelope)
  A CLOSED Malli map — unknown keys rejected:
    :grant/id            uuid
    :grant/principal     the actor the grant is issued TO (opaque
                         principal ref; tenants arrive in step 3, so
                         this is a bare identifier for now)
    :grant/effect-class  [:enum :effect/merge :effect/deploy
                          :effect/spend :effect/push :effect/pr-create]
                         - the closed set of irreversible effects
    :grant/scope         map - what the grant is bounded TO (repo, PR
                         number, namespace, workflow-run id)
    :grant/constraints   map - the ceilings: {:constraint/max-cost-usd,
                         :constraint/max-tokens, :constraint/max-count,
                         :constraint/expires-at}
    :grant/parent-id     uuid or nil - LINEAGE. A delegated grant names
                         the grant it was cut from.
    :grant/delegable?    boolean - may this grant be sub-delegated
    :grant/issued-at     inst
    :grant/expires-at    inst
    :grant/revoked-at    inst or nil
    :grant/revocation-reason keyword or nil

  GROUP 2: Lineage is enforced, not decorative
  A delegated grant MUST NOT exceed its parent on any constraint axis
  (Ariadne's attenuation rule: you cannot lend what you were not lent).
  `delegate` returns an anomaly when the child would widen scope,
  raise a ceiling, extend expiry past the parent, or when the parent
  is non-delegable or already revoked.

  GROUP 3: Revocation is a state, not a deletion
  `revoke` stamps :grant/revoked-at + :grant/revocation-reason and
  leaves the record. Revoking a parent transitively invalidates its
  descendants — `active?` walks lineage rather than trusting a flag.
  This is the representation step 2e's revocation-for-cause acts on.

  GROUP 4: Runtime-only construction
  Same label-plane rule as DecisionEnvelope (§13.4): no public API
  accepts an agent-supplied grant. A grant arriving as agent text is
  data shaped like a grant, never a grant. Docstrings state it.

  This spec builds the OBJECT ONLY. Nothing consults grants yet — that
  is 2b (decide) and 2d (call sites). Shipping the object first keeps
  the enforcement specs small and gives them a return type."

 :spec/intent {:type :feature
               :scope ["components/execution-grant/"
                       "projects/*/deps.edn"]}

 :spec/constraints
 ["Closed schema - m/validate rejects unknown keys; a test proves it"
  "No public constructor accepts an externally-supplied :grant/id or decision to grant"
  "Effect classes are a closed enum, not free-form keywords"
  "Attenuation enforced in code, not documented in prose"
  "Vocabulary (severity, enforcement, reason codes) via policy-clause interface only - no new literals"
  "Stratified design, max 3 layers per file"
  "New component MUST be added to all four projects' deps.edn or poly check fails"]

 :spec/acceptance-criteria
 ["Grant schema validates the documented shape and rejects extra keys"
  "delegate returns an anomaly when the child grant raises any constraint ceiling above its parent"
  "delegate returns an anomaly when the parent is :grant/delegable? false"
  "delegate returns an anomaly when the parent is already revoked"
  "active? returns false for a grant whose ANCESTOR was revoked, not just its own :grant/revoked-at"
  "active? returns false for an expired grant even when :grant/revoked-at is nil"
  "revoke preserves the record - the grant is still readable after revocation, with reason"
  "poly check reports 0 errors"
  "full bb test green"]

 :spec/priority
 {:tier :blocker
  :axes #{:correctness :governance-credibility :policy-enforcement}
  :theme :ariadne-grants
  :rationale "Every later step-2 spec produces or consumes a grant; decide() cannot check a constraint that has no representation."
  :blocks ["ariadne-grant-constraints-decide.spec.edn"]
  :blocked-by []}

 :spec/workflow-type :canonical-sdlc}
